Files without the bodies of their largest functions for the checkers of other files

checker.ts is 3.15 MB, 3.10 of them the body of createTypeChecker. Its parse thread reads, parses,
binds and publishes it in 45 ms, the other 248 files of the TypeScript compiler are parsed 15 to
30 ms earlier, and the checkers of those files wait for a body none of them reads.

A parse thread now leaves such a body out (Parser.skipFunctionBlock): of a function declaration at
the top of a TypeScript module, with a return type, whose body has a megabyte of text or more
(TSC_TS_SKELETON_BODY; TSC_TS_SKELETON=0 for none). The file without it takes its thread 7 ms and
stands in for itself (SourceFile.skippedBodies): the program is built and the checkers start when
the other files are parsed. The checker that takes the file parses it in full, in its own heap,
and continues with a program of its own in which the file is whole (withFullFile, over
Program.updateFile).

What keeps the diagnostics those of files parsed in full:

- Where the body ends is a guess that is checked. Nothing between the braces is read: the body is
  taken to end at the first line that starts with `}`, in a declaration that starts a line and
  whose `{` ends one. The checker's own parse says whether it does (the same statements at the top
  of the file, each from and to the same character, and no syntax error); if not, or if the file in
  full does not import what the file without the body does, the command line is run again with
  every file parsed in full (compileAgainInFull).
- The checkers of other files read the signature, not the body. A body's declarations are in scope
  in the signature as values, not as types: a signature with a typeof query or a computed property
  name, or a parameter typed by its initializer or its binding pattern, keeps its body, and so does
  a function without a return type. A file that is a module only by what a body holds is parsed in
  full.
- Only where every use of the program's files is a check on a checker thread: not when the program
  emits or reports declaration diagnostics, is incremental or watched, lists files or counts nodes,
  or has one checker; a program that is not checked after all (a syntax error elsewhere, a thread
  that failed) compiles again in full.

On the TypeScript compiler project (noEmit, ten checkers, a plain garnet build on a loaded machine,
fifteen alternating runs, with the change before this one in both): the checkers start at 45.9 ms
(minimum) and 49.9 (median) instead of 70.5 and 75.3, and the compile ends at 256.0 and 267.2 ms
instead of 269.4 and 281.5. The whole work is what it was (the body is parsed by a checker instead
of a parse thread, and the forks of that checker take more of the file); what is gone is nine
processors waiting for one file. The checker of checker.ts starts on its statements 60 ms later
than it did: delaying it by up to 70 ms in the old schedule moved the end by 1 to 3 ms, since it
shares what is left with forks of itself.

Checked with every body left out that may be (TSC_TS_SKELETON_BODY=1): the corpus on two checkers,
6,537 cases identical, a file replaced by its checker in 75; with the checker's parse made to
disagree (TSC_TS_SKELETON_FAIL=1), 6,537 identical and 75 compiled again; the compiler project,
where 69 of 78 files then stand in for themselves, under options that report 1,391 and 762
diagnostics; and eight cases of two and three files with errors in and outside bodies.

